---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import OptionsTable from "../components/api/OptionsTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "busy", label: "Marking it busy" },
  { id: "scrolling", label: "Regions that scroll" },
  { id: "options", label: "Options" },
  { id: "methods", label: "Methods" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="loading_overlay" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          Use <code>.loading-overlay</code> to cover part of a page while it loads,
          so nobody edits or presses what is about to change.
        </p>
        <p>
          Put a <a href={route('loading_indicator')}>loading indicator</a> in the
          region as a direct child, then the content. <code>AutoInit()</code> starts
          every <code>.loading-overlay</code>. While the region has
          <code>aria-busy="true"</code>, a scrim covers it, the indicator shows in its
          centre, and nothing inside takes a click, a tap or keyboard focus. Press
          Save below to see it.
        </p>
        <form id="demo-overlay" class="loading-overlay mb-3" style="max-width: 28rem; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid var(--md-sys-color-outline-variant); border-radius: 16px;">
          <span class="loading-indicator contained" role="status" aria-label="Saving"></span>
          <div class="field mb-3">
            <input id="demo-overlay-name" type="text" placeholder=" " value="Ada Lovelace">
            <label for="demo-overlay-name">Name</label>
          </div>
          <button type="submit" class="filled">Save</button>
        </form>
<Code code={`
<form class="loading-overlay">
  <span class="loading-indicator contained" role="status" aria-label="Saving"></span>
  <div class="field">
    <input id="name" type="text" placeholder=" ">
    <label for="name">Name</label>
  </div>
  <button type="submit" class="filled">Save</button>
</form>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.busy}>
        <p>
          The page sets <code>aria-busy="true"</code> on the region when the work
          starts, and removes it, or sets any other value, when the work is done.
          There is no method for either. The script watches the attribute and makes
          the region <code>inert</code> while it is busy. When the region clears, it
          gets back the <code>inert</code> state it had before.
        </p>
<Code code={`
const region = document.querySelector('.loading-overlay');
region.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');
try {
  await save();
} finally {
  region.removeAttribute('aria-busy');
}
`} />
        <p>
          Inert content leaves the accessibility tree, the indicator's own status
          included. So the script adds a visually hidden status region just after
          the region, which announces "Loading" when the region becomes busy and
          "Loaded" when it clears. Set the words with the <code>i18n</code> option.
        </p>
        <p>
          A focused control drops focus when its region turns inert. When the
          region clears, focus goes back to that control, unless the person moved
          focus somewhere else in the meantime or the page removed the control.
        </p>
        <p>
          The scrim and the indicator show from the attribute alone, before the
          script starts, but only the script blocks input. They fade in and out,
          and appear at once under reduced motion. In forced colors the scrim dims
          the content toward the system background, so it still looks unavailable.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.scrolling}>
        <p>
          The scrim scrolls with the content of a region that scrolls itself, so
          it would only cover the first screenful. Put the overlay on a wrapper
          around the scroller instead. The region becomes a positioning context for
          the scrim, with <code>position: relative</code>, unless a utility such as
          <code>.sticky-top</code> positions it already.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="loading-overlay">
  <span class="loading-indicator contained" role="status" aria-label="Loading"></span>
  <div style="height: 24rem; overflow-y: auto">...rows</div>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.options}>
        <OptionsTable>
          <tr>
            <td><code>i18n</code></td>
            <td>Object</td>
            <td><code>{"{ loading: 'Loading', loaded: 'Loaded' }"}</code></td>
            <td>What the status region announces when the region becomes busy and when it clears.</td>
          </tr>
        </OptionsTable>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.methods}>
<Code code={`
const overlay = Expressive.LoadingOverlay.getInstance(document.querySelector('.loading-overlay'));
overlay.destroy(); // stops following aria-busy, removes the status region, gives inert back
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <p>Override these on the <code>.loading-overlay</code>.</p>
        <TokenTable>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-loading-overlay-scrim-color</code></td><td>surface at 70%</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-loading-overlay-motion</code></td><td>default effects spring</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-loading-overlay-motion-duration</code></td><td>the default effects spring's duration</td></tr>
        </TokenTable>
  </Section>
<script is:inline>
(() => {
  const form = document.getElementById('demo-overlay');
  form.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    form.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');
    setTimeout(() => form.removeAttribute('aria-busy'), 2000);
  });
})();
</script>
</DocsLayout>
